# The History of an Ideal's Growth
Tamil title: இலட்சியம் வளர்ந்த வரலாறு

This speech was delivered by Perarignar Anna on 27 August 1944, proclaiming the principle “Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians.”

## The Ideal Is Our Fundamental Slogan

The ideal “Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians” has now become our fundamental slogan. All other questions receive attention only insofar as they seek support for this ideal. We hold it as our single objective. Although members of the Dravidar Kazhagam possess many different views and programmes on religious, social and economic questions, all of them are arranged, like the *sruti* in music, to accord with the ideal “Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians.”

## A Day for Affirming Our Resolve

Dravida Nadu Separation Day is being arranged to declare our resolve never to abandon this ideal and to state categorically that we cannot be satisfied by small coins thrown in our path before it is achieved. On this occasion, we wish to call attention to the manner in which the ideal “Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians” developed.

## The Hero Who Died Proclaiming a Right

The assertion of the right to be called Dravidian arose here as early as thirty years ago. But contact with mansion-dwellers corrupted that cry. Dr C. Natesa Mudaliar proclaimed before the people, until his final breath, that this country is Dravida Nadu and that we who live here are Dravidians.

The noble movement he began created great momentum. Yet the wealthy men who sat in palaces and conducted politics did not use it for true liberation. They used it to obtain titles and offices, and intended to devote all their ability to criticising Congress while simultaneously singing praise of the British.

Thus the term of right, “Dravidian,” did not become a heroic song for establishing a Dravidian Government; it became a selfish noise knocking on the door of a Government office. Dr Natesa Mudaliar died broken-hearted. As long as he lived, he never failed to speak as a Dravidian.

## Leaders Who Showed No Zeal to Remove Aryanism

In those days, the names S.I.L.F. and Justice circulated in English within mansions, but before the people the name “Dravidian” alone was used. The Tamil newspaper itself was named *Dravidan*. Many people came forward in various fields to work for a Dravidian renaissance, speaking of Dravidian art, Dravidian religion, Dravidian medicine and Dravidian musicians.

But the leaders failed to show interest in that foundational work. They spent all their energy chasing the illusory deer called political power. When they met prominent citizens in every city, they spoke only about elections. They stressed that Iyers dominated Government employment and that this must be abolished, but they did not work to remove the Aryanism embedded in society or instil Dravidian consciousness.

## The Leaders of “Those Days” Became a Puzzle to the People

The leaders of those days openly said that Brahmin domination in society was not dangerous and that only Brahmins possessing political dominance endangered us. Such was their speech.

With the exception of one or two such as Dr Natesa Mudaliar, activists who mingled with the people and carried out propaganda—J. N. Ramanathan, Kannappar and M. V. Subramanian among them—instilled Dravidian consciousness and explained and condemned Brahmin domination not merely in politics but in society. They urged the people to live as Dravidians.

The leaders, however, used the people only to the extent of making them cast votes against Brahmins. At first the people did not understand those leaders' conduct. After understanding it, they disliked it; because they disliked it, they withdrew.

## Mansion-Dwellers Hunting the Illusory Deer

The first attempt to build Dravidian ethnic consciousness was defeated—not because the people lacked interest, but because our leaders attended only to politics and considered intervention in society a mistake.

Twenty years were spent hunting the illusory deer: arranging ministerial offices, which is to say pursuing self-interest, and consequently engaging in conspiratorial consultations. Mansions became halls of conspiracy. Political brokers became stage-managers of politics. Office-seekers mounted the stage. There were celebrations even for the foreigner.

The people clearly understood that this business was not for them and began to condemn the party. Conspiracy in the mansions grew uncontrollable. Each leader made overthrowing another his profession; they struck one another and fell in factions.

Amid that shock, the Dravidian consciousness that had initially arisen with enthusiasm faded; it did not die. That feeling has never died, though it has often faded, because it is the speech of the heart, not the cry of political locusts.

## Dravidam Suffering in Aryanism's Embrace

For a long time, Dravidam has repeatedly tried to free itself from Aryanism's embrace. Though inactive at times, it has never surrendered completely. Movements that periodically arose under various headings—Pure Tamil, Vellala civilisation, true Saivism and ancient civilisation—were all small editions of the original effort.

Each was necessary and produced some benefit. Yet because each could be understood only by a few people, these movements could not strengthen the original effort. Each became a separate movement. They began to appear contradictory, and even hostile feeling grew. Many camps arose in Tamil Nadu; the original endeavour was forgotten.

## Opponents Rejoiced at the Supposed Death of the Feeling

The country resembled a blindfolded warrior walking along a road, colliding painfully with tree after tree. It became doubtful whether Dravidian consciousness would arise once more. Opponents rejoiced that it had died altogether.

But the ideal had not died. It waited for its moment. Many moments came, but they were not the right moment.

## Fallen Tamil Nadu Found Its Own Zeal

The anti-Hindi agitation became the proper moment. It was a period when not merely anger against Congress—that was the lesser element—but love of Tamil spread throughout the country. People everywhere began reading Sangam works. They took pride in saying that the Tamil possessed a distinct language and culture. Discussion of the Five Great Epics reached public platforms.

As enthusiasm for Tamil increased, mythologists also entered the field. But before their dominance could grow, Tamil enthusiasts began investigating why the Tamil language had been brought low, Tamil culture had disappeared and Tamil Nadu's distinction had declined. Their inquiry brought them back to the point from which the effort had departed: it made them stand near the grave of Dr Natesa Mudaliar. Old memories and old heroic songs began to circulate again.

## The Warriors Who Cut a Canal So the Withered Tamil Crop Could Flourish

The sight of more than a thousand people going to prison to protect the Tamil language created a new feeling in Tamil hearts. Tamils shed tears over the graves of Thalamuthu and Natarajan. Standing beside those graves and reflecting that two young men had died and many others gone to prison, all for the protection of Tamil, Tamils formed the resolve that this condition must change.

Only then came the question: “Comrade, you cry that another language threatens Tamil. Think about why that danger arose and who caused it. Is language alone endangered? Danger has come to your art, moral order, culture, people, country and life. Have you understood it? Do you know who caused it and why? I shall tell you. Listen.

“Tamil Nadu is not in Tamil hands. Therefore Tamil wealth is not in Tamil hands. Whatever outsiders think now becomes law here. This country becomes a market for the outsider's goods.

“This condition must change. A language struggle alone is not enough. This is not the end, but the beginning. Driving out Hindi alone is insufficient. Every evil power dominating Tamil Nadu must disappear. That must become our slogan, our struggle and our ideal: Tamil Nadu for the Tamils!”

Periyar Ramasamy raised this cry. “Yes, yes! We understand; we see clearly,” cried the huge crowd, nearing a lakh, gathered on the beach that day, and they took their resolve. The sea roared as though expressing the people's anguish. Above, the moon seemed to illuminate the awakened condition of the Tamils, while the clouds dimming the sky resembled stains upon Tamil society.

The Tamil had regained his ideal. He proclaimed “Tamil Nadu for the Tamils.” The faded ideal began to move again. This time it went directly to the people's assembly and therefore gained new strength.

The people became eager to labour for an ideal. They thought: not for a mansion, not for office, but for our country's separate right shall we fight. That thought gained further strength through the golden words of the revolutionary poet:

Let us dance, let us sing the *pallu*;
Let us gather in the majesty of Tamil rule.

Joy began to dance in the eyes of thousands of young people. Elections, legislatures and ministries became very ordinary objects in their view. Those who hankered after such things began to appear as caricatures in the eyes of Salem's youth. Overcoming obstacles and hardship, young people walked toward the ideal with elation that a new path had been found.

The battle cry “Tamil Nadu for the Tamils” expressed the Tamil's discovery that he must redeem the country to protect his language and art.

## Those Who Mocked Saw a Volcano

“A rat-hole for the rats,” said intellectuals who imagined mockery could abolish anything. But the people began to utter “Tamil Nadu for the Tamils.” “Is it not true, just and necessary?” they asked, and as they spoke they reflected further upon Tamil Nadu's present condition.

They saw everything Aryanised. They investigated when this condition began and learned that it dated from the day the Tamil gave Aryanism room. They examined what existed before it and discovered beautiful Dravidam flourishing with strength, protected by sea on three sides and the Vindhyas in the north.

They saw that one part of Dravidam had become Andhra and another Kerala, yet discovered a linguistic kinship between them despite the separation. They remembered ancient Dravidam. They found that the word “Dravidam” stood as the opposite of “Aryan,” a sign denoting a culture wholly different from the culture called Aryan. History, geography and the study of collective psychology all supported this truth.

“Ah, we have found the truth,” they said. “Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians.” The ideal attained its complete form.

Today some people approach the ideal while still feeling shy to say “Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians”; they speak instead of provincial freedom and provincial autonomy. Some say, “Full rights for us, but do we not need an all-India connection?” The twists and bends in their wording are not what chiefly demand our attention. What matters is that they are walking toward the ideal.

## After His Delirium Cleared, He Refused to Think

Choosing the occasion of our Dravidian Separation Day, the leader of the Tamil Nadu Congress said, “The proposal that Dravida Nadu become a separate country can only divide the country further.” Friend Kamaraj thus describes what he believes the result of our proposal will be, but refuses to consider why it is needed. Perhaps he considered it, discovered the truth and now conceals it.

When northern leaders interfered in our affairs, how angry Kamaraj became with the high command. He should consider whether it is wrong for us to become angry when that same high command enters and dominates domestic affairs, art, civilisation and, most importantly, the economy.

Although he may rarely find time amid his many troubles, if he makes a little time and speaks to our leader, he can learn why we say Dravida Nadu must be a separate country. It would then be appropriate for him to express his view. It is not proper to examine an issue by first imagining its result oneself.

The day inviting Kamaraj and others to consider this issue and investigate its causes is July [the numeral is damaged in OCR].

## Why Did the Desire for Separate Sovereignty Arise?

The man who says Dravidastan would divide the country nevertheless comes near our ideal at the same time, knowingly or unknowingly. He has said, “Andhra, Kerala and Tamil Nadu should become separate autonomous units and live together within the Indian federation.” He calls them separately empowered units.

Why did that desire arise? What causes the demand for separate rights? What difference exists between the present condition and the condition that would result from obtaining them? He does not explain.

Nor does he reveal that contrary to his “desire,” these “units” are now being partitioned within the Constituent Assembly, since he cannot prevent it. He seeks separate sovereignty; we seek it in an unblemished form. He says that even after obtaining it, we must join the Indian federation.

Not merely an Indian federation: whether it is the Asian federation Nehru describes or the world federation Wendell Willkie proposed, we shall join. There is neither wrong nor obstacle in that. But separate sovereignty is essential. Where is the plan for it? They are already drawing up a plan in the Constituent Assembly, but it cannot fulfil Kamaraj's desire. What does our friend say to that?

## We Hear Even Opponents Speak the Ideal

Before saying Dravidastan must not exist, Congress leaders in responsible positions must ask why it is demanded and learn the answer. The celebration day is an invitation to do so.

We rejoice to see the outline of our ideal even in the words of friends who say they oppose it. What has caused this condition? The ideal has grown. We perceive its fragrance in unexpected places. If for some further time we explain our ideal, and especially its economic reasons, “Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians” will cease to be a party slogan and become the country's slogan.

The ideal that has grown through many obstacles will certainly appear in this full form, because it is the drumbeat of rights sounded by an awakened people, not party talk.

## We Have Endured; Later We Shall Gird Ourselves

Whenever pictures of Ramakrishna printed in Calcutta reach us and we drive a nail sent by the Tata company into a wall plastered with Dalmia cement to hang them, the ideal stands near our ear and gently asks, “Is this your condition? Is there no iron in your country?” Then it laughs.

When dhotis and saris from Sholapur and Bombay come here in greater numbers because they cannot go to Lahore and Peshawar, the ideal comes to mind. Medicines from the Bengal Chemical Works remind us of the ideal. When our overseas trade and contacts end with those above the Vindhyas, the ideal comes to mind.

At first there will be some hesitation; then courage will arise. From places we never expected, the ideal will be voiced: “We cannot endure this northern domination; Dravida Nadu must belong to the Dravidians.”

To see that day, we endure the humiliations, accusations, opposition and mockery of these days. We shall endure them. We are not what matters here; the country is.

---NOTES---
This is a historical political speech from 1944. Its terminology concerning Aryan, Dravidian and Brahmin identity is translated in the source's own argumentative context and is not silently modernised.

The English abbreviation OCRed as “5.1.1...” is likely S.I.L.F., the South Indian Liberal Federation, associated with the Justice Party. The translation uses that probable reading while preserving the Tamil source.

The verse beginning “ஆடுவமே பள்ளுப் பாடுவமே” is attributed in the speech to the revolutionary poet; its rhythm and political meaning are translated conservatively.

The numeral in the Separation Day date “ஐூலை /-ந் தேதி” is damaged, and the English-like string before “தனி உரிமையுள்ள அங்கங்கள்” is illegible. Neither is silently reconstructed.
